# Autonomous AI Development Rules

## 1. Core Principles
- The human's role is strictly limited to "defining requirements" and "deciding on PR merges."
- The agent must autonomously handle the entire cycle: reviewing issues, creating branches, implementing code, verifying changes, and opening PRs.

## 2. Issue Creation Rules
- When instructed to create an issue via chat, use the `gh issue create` command.
- Title prefixes: `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`
- The body must include: "Purpose," "Implementation Requirements," and "Acceptance Criteria."

## 3. Branching & Development Rules
- Always branch off from the latest `dev` branch.
- Branch naming convention: `issue-<number>` (例: `issue-7`)
- Execute commands non-interactively (no prompts waiting for user input) and default to `pnpm`.

## 4. Verification & Quality Assurance Rules
- After code changes, always run the following locally and ensure it passes:
  `pnpm lint && pnpm build`
- If errors occur, analyze the error logs autonomously and iterate on fixes until all checks pass.

## 5. Pull Request Rules
- Always set the target (base) branch to `dev` (`--base dev`).
- Match the PR title to the issue title.
- The first line of the PR body must state: `Closes #<issue-number>`
- Command template:
  `gh pr create --base dev --title "..." --body "Closes #<number> ... [Summary and Test Results]"`

## 6. TypeScript & React Coding Conventions
- Standardize on using `Array<Type>` instead of `Type[]` for array type declarations (e.g., `Array<string>`, `Array<DmmItem>`).
- Direct declaration of `children: React.ReactNode` is prohibited. Always use `PropsWithChildren` or `PropsWithChildren<Props>` for React components accepting children.


